--TEST--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` accepts ADR 0006's second entry spelling — a static method reference — and it reaches the same body the path form does rather than any compile question — ADR 0083 §§ 1-2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0083 § 2 makes the operand `spawn script`'s, and ADR 0006 § *Decision*
// gives that operand two spellings: a file path, and a static method whose
// parameters `args:` binds by name. This case is the accepted half of the rule
// at this second site; `an-upgrade-refuses-an-entry-it-cannot-see-the-function-
// behind.nvst` is the refused half, and the two together are the whole of it.
class Chat {
    public static function run(string $room): void {
        echo "ran ", $room, "\n";
    }
}

// § 1's root isolate is not built, so what says the entry was *accepted* is
// that the member reaches its own body and reports from there — byte for byte
// the report the path form gets, never a diagnostic and never a silent `void`.
try {
    Core\Socket::upgrade(Chat::run(...), args: {room: "lobby"});
    echo "upgraded\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// The entry is *named*, not called: `ran lobby` is nowhere above, because the
// isolate § 1 opens is the only place that body ever runs.
echo "not run here\n";
--EXPECT--
`Core\Socket::upgrade` cannot open a connection yet: the root isolate a WebSocket connection runs in is not built, so there is nothing to hand this connection to
not run here
